<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Plan 53 §8 M0 / §5.2. THE RECORD MODE OF A NEW PROJECT IS SEEDED FROM THE
  USER'S INI, AND A LOADED PROJECT IS NOT.

  THIS CASE OWNS THE `midi/recordMode` INI KEY from now on. set-option's rule
  is one owning case per key (docs/ACTIONS.md), because set-option writes the
  developer's real smaragd.ini. It is RUN_SERIAL, it restores the key to its
  SOpt default at the end, and NO OTHER CASE MAY READ OR WRITE IT.
  midi_record_modes.qxa, which used to drive that key, no longer touches it.

  WHY THE KEY IS `midi/recordMode` AND NOT A NEW ONE. It predates plan 53 and
  already means exactly this — "how should a recorded pass combine with what is
  there" — in a three-value vocabulary (new-take | overdub | replace). Plan 53
  keeps it as the SEED for a new project and maps it: new-take and anything
  unknown land on `takes`, the other two on themselves. `takes-split` has no
  INI spelling because no release has offered it.

  WHAT THE LOAD HALF CAN AND CANNOT PROVE, measured rather than assumed.
  SProject's property load MERGES over whatever the object already holds, so a
  seed written in fileOpen's `new SProject()` would survive for every file that
  LACKS the key — every project saved before plan 53 would then read the user's
  INI value instead of `takes`. That is why the seed runs ONLY at fileNew() and
  at close-project's fresh project.

  The obvious gate for it — load a fixture with no recordMode key and assert
  `takes` — DOES NOT WORK, and the reason is worth knowing:
  SLoadProjectAction::apply( SProject *project ) loads INTO THE CURRENT
  PROJECT. It clears arrangements and not properties, so the in-memory value
  survives a load whatever the file says. fileOpen() does not behave that way
  — it loads into a fresh SProject — so no .qxa can reach fileOpen's
  semantics, and a case asserting `takes` there would be asserting the verb's
  merge, not the seed's placement. Filed separately rather than papered over.

  So this case gates the SUBSTANCE instead: a file that STATES a mode wins over
  the seed. Save a project at `overdub`, close to a fresh one seeded `replace`,
  load the file back, and the mode must be `overdub`. An implementation that
  seeded over a load would read `replace` there and fail.

  close-project is what makes any of this gateable without a GUI: it builds its
  replacement through the REAL new-project path.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="record_mode_ini_seed">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <!-- The project the runner made was built before this case set anything,
         so it carries whatever the ambient INI said. Nothing is asserted about
         it; the case starts measuring after it has set the key itself. -->
    <set-option key="midi/recordMode" value="replace"/>

    <!-- A FRESH project through the real new-project path picks the seed up. -->
    <close-project/>
    <assert-record-mode project="replace"/>

    <!-- ...and a lane with no attribute of its own resolves to it. -->
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <assert-record-mode trackPath="0" own="" resolved="replace"/>

    <!-- A FILE THAT STATES A MODE WINS OVER THE SEED. Saved at `overdub`,
         reloaded into a project the INI seeded `replace`. -->
    <set-property key="recordMode" value='["overdub"]'/>
    <assert-record-mode project="overdub"/>
    <save-project path="../../build/test-output/record_mode_ini_seed/rmis.qxp"/>

    <close-project/>
    <assert-record-mode project="replace"/>

    <load-project path="../../build/test-output/record_mode_ini_seed/rmis.qxp"/>
    <assert-record-mode project="overdub"/>

    <!-- overdub maps to itself through the seed. -->
    <set-option key="midi/recordMode" value="overdub"/>
    <close-project/>
    <assert-record-mode project="overdub"/>

    <!-- new-take is the INI's own spelling for plan 53's `takes`, and because
         that EQUALS the default the seed writes no property at all — which is
         what keeps a new project byte-identical to a pre-plan-53 one. -->
    <set-option key="midi/recordMode" value="new-take"/>
    <close-project/>
    <assert-record-mode project="takes"/>

    <!-- An INI value no build understands lands on the default rather than
         pinning the project to a mode no planner knows. -->
    <set-option key="midi/recordMode" value="nonsense"/>
    <close-project/>
    <assert-record-mode project="takes"/>

    <!-- RESTORE THE KEY to its SOpt default, so the INI comes back
         byte-identical and the next case finds what it expects. -->
    <set-option key="midi/recordMode" value="new-take"/>
  </actions>

</SActionScript>
